    <post id="post-1" date-created="2026-08-07T10:00:00Z" date-modified="2026-08-07T12:00:00Z" approved="true" post-url="https://endjin.com/blog/writing-effective-copilot-instructions-for-complex-codebases" type="normal" hasexcerpt="true" views="1500">
      <title type="text">Writing Effective Copilot Instructions for Complex Codebases</title>
      <excerpt type="html">&lt;p&gt;Modular skill files beat one monolithic instructions document once a repository stops fitting in context.&lt;/p&gt;</excerpt>
      <content type="html">&lt;p&gt;A single instructions file is read in full, every time. Past a certain repository size that is neither affordable nor useful.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;h2&gt;What works better&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;Split guidance by task, not by subsystem&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;State what the build gate actually checks&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Record the reasoning, so a rule can be revisited&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;p&gt;Guidance for an assistant is documentation with a consumer, and it rots the same way.&lt;/p&gt;</content>
      <post-name type="text">writing-effective-copilot-instructions-for-complex-codebases</post-name>
      <authors>
        <author ref="author-1"/>
      </authors>
      <categories>
        <category ref="cat-1"/>
        <category ref="cat-2"/>
      </categories>
      <comments>
        <comment id="comment-1" date-created="2026-08-07T11:00:00Z" date-modified="2026-08-07T11:00:00Z" approved="true" user-name="Jess Panni" user-email="hello@endjin.com" user-url="https://endjin.com/who-we-are/">
          <title type="text">The skill-file split</title>
          <content type="text">The by-task split is the part that generalises. We reached the same conclusion from the opposite direction.</content>
        </comment>
        <comment id="comment-2" date-created="2026-08-07T11:30:00Z" date-modified="2026-08-07T11:30:00Z" approved="true" user-name="Ed Freeman" user-email="hello@endjin.com">
          <content type="text">Worth adding: state the command, not just the rule. A gate nobody can run is a gate nobody runs.</content>
        </comment>
        <comment id="comment-3" date-created="2026-08-07T12:00:00Z" date-modified="2026-08-07T12:00:00Z" approved="false" user-name="Unmoderated Commenter">
          <content type="text">An unapproved comment. It exists so BlogMLApprovalStatus.NotApproved is reached on load.</content>
        </comment>
